Subject: [PATCH qemu-server 0/5] vga: D-Bus display, render node and WebRTC console attach

This series lets a VM export its display over QEMU's D-Bus display
(peer-to-peer, no bus daemon) and lets a WebRTC console attach to it
through the API, as discussed in the RFC "WebRTC console for VMs via
QEMU's D-Bus display" on this list (feature request: bug #8106).

Patch 1 stands on its own: it adds 'rendernode' to the VGA option so a
VirGL guest can be placed on a chosen GPU (bug #4771).

Patches 2-4 add the 'dbus' flag (-display dbus,p2p=yes, GL for
virtio-gl), the webrtcproxy API call that attaches a new D-Bus client
(getfd + add_client) and hands it with the browser's offer to the
node's WebRTC console service (a separate package, over a root-owned
unix socket with SCM_RIGHTS), and the status flags the web UI needs.

Patch 5 adds qemu-vdagent (clipboard=on) for a D-Bus display, so the
console shares the clipboard with a guest running spice-vdagent, as
the VNC clipboard does.  Note that spice-vdagent is an X11 client: on
a Wayland desktop (e.g. KDE Plasma) it reaches X11 applications only.

With GL, QEMU refuses VNC and SPICE next to the D-Bus display, so such
a VM has only the WebRTC console; vncproxy and spiceproxy say so.

Tested on PVE 9.2 (qemu-server 9.2.7 plus this series, pve-qemu-kvm
11.0) with an RTX 3080: a Kubuntu 26.04 guest with
'vga: virtio-gl,dbus=1' streams 1280x800 at 60 fps through
webrtcproxy (NVENC H.264), VM.Console is enforced (403 without it),
the console's clipboard reaches QEMU and the vdagent channel in both
directions, and config-to-command tests cover all new command lines.

Dmitry R (5):
  fix #4771: vga: add rendernode option for virtio-gl
  vga: add option to export the display over D-Bus
  api: add webrtcproxy to attach a WebRTC console to the D-Bus display
  api: status: report a D-Bus display to consoles
  vga: share the clipboard of a D-Bus display with the guest

